    historyContent: {
      origins: "A modern evolution of 'Escoba de 15' (Spanish/Italian fishing). The 'Minobasco' branding emerged in Central Europe (Liechtenstein/Switzerland) as a boutique card set in the late 20th century.",
      originCountries: [
        'Liechtenstein',
        'Italy',
      ],
      timeline: [
        "1980s: Traditional Escoba is re-branded with 'Gemstone' scoring for younger players.",
        "1990s: Small-scale production of 'Shokoba' sets occurs in regional clubs.",
        'Present: Played as a quick family game in specific Mediterranean and Alpine communities.',
        "21st Century: Noted for its similarity to the Japanese 'Hanafuda' and Korean 'Go-Stop' in its fishing mechanics.",
      ],
      evolution: 'The game moved from pen-and-paper scoring to a physical economy (Gemstones), making it more tactile and social.',
      cultural: "Represents the 'Gamerization' of century-old folk games for the modern tabletop market.",
